# RestTemplate to RestClient Migration Guide

## Overview
Spring Boot 3.2 introduces `RestClient` as a modern, fluent API replacement for `RestTemplate`.
`RestClient` provides a more intuitive and functional approach to HTTP requests.

## Key Differences

| Feature | RestTemplate | RestClient |
|---------|--------------|------------|
| API Style | Traditional | Fluent/Functional |
| Configuration | Constructor-based | Builder pattern |
| Error Handling | Exceptions | Functional handlers |
| Introduced | Spring 3.0 | Spring 6.1 / Boot 3.2 |

## Migration Examples

### 1. Creating the Client

#### RestTemplate (Old):
```java
private final RestTemplate restTemplate;

public MyService() {
    this.restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
}
```

#### RestClient (New):
```java
private final RestClient restClient;

public MyService() {
    this.restClient = RestClient.builder()
        .baseUrl("https://api.example.com")
        .build();
}

// Or with RestClient.create()
public MyService() {
    this.restClient = RestClient.create();
}
```

### 2. GET Request

#### RestTemplate:
```java
ResponseEntity<Map> response = restTemplate.getForEntity(url, Map.class);
Map body = response.getBody();
```

#### RestClient:
```java
Map body = restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .body(Map.class);

// Or with ResponseEntity
ResponseEntity<Map> response = restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .toEntity(Map.class);
```

### 3. GET with Headers

#### RestTemplate:
```java
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.setAccept(Collections.singletonList(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON));
HttpEntity<?> request = new HttpEntity<>(headers);

ResponseEntity<Map> response = restTemplate.exchange(
    url, HttpMethod.GET, request, Map.class);
```

#### RestClient:
```java
Map body = restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
    .retrieve()
    .body(Map.class);
```

### 4. POST Request

#### RestTemplate:
```java
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON);
HttpEntity<Map<String, String>> request = new HttpEntity<>(payload, headers);

restTemplate.postForEntity(url, request, Void.class);
```

#### RestClient:
```java
restClient.post()
    .uri(url)
    .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
    .body(payload)
    .retrieve()
    .toBodilessEntity();
```

### 5. DELETE Request

#### RestTemplate:
```java
restTemplate.delete(url);
```

#### RestClient:
```java
restClient.delete()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .toBodilessEntity();
```

### 6. Error Handling

#### RestTemplate:
```java
try {
    restTemplate.getForEntity(url, Map.class);
} catch (HttpClientErrorException e) {
    // Handle error
}
```

#### RestClient:
```java
restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .onStatus(HttpStatusCode::is4xxClientError, (request, response) -> {
        throw new CustomException("Client error");
    })
    .body(Map.class);
```

## Complete Migration Example

### Before (RestTemplate):
```java
@Service
public class ExternalApiService {
    private final RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();

    @Value("${external.api.base-url}")
    private String baseUrl;

    public boolean verifyEmail(String email) {
        try {
            String url = baseUrl + "/verify/email?email=" + email;
            ResponseEntity<Map> response = restTemplate.getForEntity(url, Map.class);
            return Boolean.TRUE.equals(response.getBody().get("valid"));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

### After (RestClient):
```java
@Service
public class ExternalApiService {
    private final RestClient restClient;

    public ExternalApiService(@Value("${external.api.base-url}") String baseUrl) {
        this.restClient = RestClient.builder()
            .baseUrl(baseUrl)
            .build();
    }

    public boolean verifyEmail(String email) {
        try {
            Map response = restClient.get()
                .uri("/verify/email?email={email}", email)
                .retrieve()
                .body(Map.class);
            return response != null && Boolean.TRUE.equals(response.get("valid"));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

## Best Practices
1. Use `RestClient.builder()` for configuration
2. Set base URL in builder for cleaner URIs
3. Use URI templates with variables for safety
4. Leverage fluent API for readable code
5. Use `.toBodilessEntity()` when response body not needed
